# Use the right tone

Write Civica text so it is:

- specific
- informative
- clear and concise
- brisk, but not curt
- precise, but human: friendliness can add vague, extra words, but the text should never sound like a machine
- serious, but not self-important
- free of emotion: adjectives are a matter of opinion and can sound like a sales pitch

Write conversationally, as you would speak to one person in the home, and sound like someone who can help them. Use the [active voice](clear-language.html#use-the-active-voice).

You rarely need “please” or “please note”, even in an instruction. Write “Restart Home Assistant”, not “Please restart Home Assistant”.

Do not write long text in capital letters. Capitals slow reading down and come across as shouting.

<h2 id="match-the-words-to-what-is-known">Match the words to what is known</h2>

<h3 id="wg-011">Say only what Home Assistant knows</h3>

A reading or a request is not a fact. Say what the device reported: “The front door lock reports Locked”, not “The front door is locked”.

<h3 id="wg-012">Be calm without false reassurance</h3>

Give the fact and the next step. Do not joke or say “do not worry”: “The lamp has not reported its new state. Check the lamp is plugged in, then try again.”

<h3 id="wg-046">Do not blame or raise false alarms</h3>

Say what the sensor reports, not what someone did wrong: “The bedroom window sensor has reported Open for 40 minutes”, not “You left the window open again”. Keep urgent wording for real alarms, such as smoke or a water leak. If every message is urgent, people stop reading them.

## Do not use offensive language

Civica text must never contain offensive language. Do not swear, and do not use words in an offensive way about:

- race
- ethnicity
- nationality
- religion
- disability
- mental health
- gender identity
- sexual orientation
- body parts
- sexual references

The one exception is a name that someone in the home typed. Show it exactly as they typed it.

For help choosing words, read [Use respectful, specific language](clear-language.html#wg-062) and [Inclusive content](../inclusive-content/index.html).

## Address the user directly

Speak to the reader as “you” wherever you can.

Civica text often asks someone to do something. Ask them directly: “Turn off the heating” or “You can change this in **Settings**”.

When you write about someone else, use “they”: “They can turn on the porch light”, not “He or she can turn on the porch light”.

<h3 id="wg-066">Treat presence as sensitive and uncertain</h3>

When you write about someone in the home, say what their device reported, not where they are: “Alex’s phone last reported Away at 17:40.” Do not show presence on a shared screen unless the household chose to show it.

## Make it clear who did it

Before you write “it” or “we”, name who acted:

- Home Assistant, for something it did by itself
- the automation, by its name
- the person, for something someone in the home did

Write “Home Assistant turned off the heating at 22:00”, not “We turned off the heating”.
